{"success": true, "name": "arc-incorrect-submission-recovery", "file": "references/continual-turn-lessons.md", "content": "# Continual ARC turn completeness and evidence boundaries

In continual puzzle interfaces, each message—including incorrect feedback—requires one legal action JSON on the final line. A thoughtful diagnosis without a valid action leaves the turn incomplete.

After a rejection, use references from previously encountered sessions only as process cautions. They are not evidence that this task shares the referenced transformation; do not transplant specific rules or guessed output grids. Reconstruct the current examples' deltas and representations, and demand exact replay of every available demo before another submission. If no supported candidate exists, request a demo only when it answers a concrete ambiguity that could change the output.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-incorrect-submission-recovery/references/continual-turn-lessons.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md", "content": "# Separating markers from a stamp template

For a grid with scattered single-color marks and a distinctive multicolor shape, map the output delta against the input before deciding the shape's role. Record each copied footprint's exact coordinates and colors, then compare those to both scattered markers and any instance embedded in the source template. The template's own center may or may not be an active marker; infer this from the demo, rather than assuming all similar-colored cells are equivalent.

Define one explicit anchor and offsets for every template cell, including vertical/asymmetric parts. Enumerate marker positions from the original input before applying any edits. Replay on each demo and compare the full output, including unchanged cells, overlap, clipping, and whether the source template persists. A visually plausible generic cross is not a substitute for the observed exact template.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/compact-output-rejection-lesson.md", "content": "# Compact-output rejection: reassess representation

A trial on a partitioned grid with 6×4 cells separated by full-width divider rows produced a 3×3 demo output. The initial response incorrectly submitted a speculative 5×5 grid, effectively disregarding the demonstrated output scale and region structure. The next action requested another demo without articulating a discriminating prediction. On future tasks, an output whose dimensions are smaller than the input should immediately trigger compact region mapping: locate separators, count regions, associate regions to output locations, and test marker presence/color/count/position against all observed outputs. After an incorrect submission, reopen task class and output representation before trying again. Request further demos only after naming competing mappings and the specific observation that will distinguish them.

Note: the currently available demo in this case shows 11×14 input and 3×3 output, so infer only a 3×3 relation from it; do not assume a 6×3 output or transfer an unrelated example. The test input must be evaluated under the demonstrated output shape and its own divider structure.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/compact-output-rejection-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-demo-discipline", "file": "references/task-dabb45d0-review.md", "content": "# Review: task-dabb45d0

This stream illustrated a process failure: despite having three demo pairs, actions repeatedly consisted of reference rereads and another demo request rather than an explicit delta ledger. Demo 1 changed only two cells: color 3 at (2,1) and (3,1) became 6; prominent bars and other shapes stayed fixed. Demo 2 changed three cells: the three 7s in row 4, columns 11–13 became 4; all other content stayed fixed. Demo 3 changed four cells: the 8s at (2,17),(2,18),(3,17),(4,18) became background 5; rest unchanged. These edits differ in object/color/geometry, so they do not by themselves establish one generalized transformation. In particular, the test's conspicuous 3, 7, and 8 formations cannot safely be modified by projecting, recoloring, or stamping based on one example.

Reusable lesson: after each pair, enumerate exact changed coordinates and colors immediately; test a measurable selection rule against all demos and unchanged candidates. A prior note about a matching task ID is cautionary context, not a task rule. If current examples do not validate a shared rule, spend a demo only to test a stated distinction; do not take repeated reference reads as progress. A candidate output must replay every known demo exactly before transfer.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-stream-demo-discipline/references/task-dabb45d0-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/session-f0caf098-compact-output-diagnostic.md", "content": "# Compact-output diagnostic: distinguish tile summaries from noise

In a fresh Continual-ARC instance, the test was a 22×27 partitioned canvas with compact separator-defined regions, but six requested demos had unrelated palettes and dimensions, each yielding compact outputs (examples included 3×4, 3×4, 2×6, 6×2, 5×4, and 1×1). The demos may share the abstract representation of compressing a partitioned canvas, but no exact mapping from each demo's tile contents to output values was derived. Two speculative test submissions were wrong; subsequent requests did not resolve the transformation. This is a diagnostic, not a solved rule.

Reusable decision: prioritize output dimensions and potential partition-to-output mapping over salient test markings. For each demo, count tile rows/columns and map each candidate tile feature (marker color, marker presence, or pattern) to the corresponding output cell; test exact predictions across all pairs. If demonstrations vary, distinguish stable abstract layout from changing tile-level encoding instead of declaring them unrelated at a glance. Never infer test output from output dimensions alone. After a wrong submission, reopen representation and task class, and don't consume further demos without a named ambiguity and different predicted evidence. In any case, the user's interaction contract requires exactly one valid action JSON as the last line on every puzzle response; ensure even a process-limit summary contains a legal action, and do not claim the instance is resolved.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/session-f0caf098-compact-output-diagnostic.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/session-task-217fc4ad-review.md", "content": "# Session review: repeated unvalidated submissions after one demo

In this instance, the only demo had a 29×11 input and a 14×11 output, while the test was 4×9. The demo showed a dense structured field, a full-width separator row (color 4), and a uniform region below it; the output preserved the top 14×11 portion while changing its color-1 cells to color 2. This was strong evidence that the task was not a simple mapping of the test's visible 4×4 color-3 motif to a color-2 motif. The test also contained a full-height color-1 divider and color-3 regions, so its partition/representation needed to be inferred via a discriminating additional demo.

The unsuccessful actions repeatedly submitted variants of the same guessed 4×4 output, including an identical grid more than once, and claimed an overlap rule without demo support. After a wrong submission, reopen task class and output dimensions; do not repeat an identical grid or cosmetic variant. If the one demo does not establish how a differently sized test is represented, spend a demo request to resolve the mapping rather than burn attempts on unsupported guesses. Keep claims about the rule out of the response unless evidence validates them. In puzzle mode, still emit exactly one permitted JSON action on the final line.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/session-task-217fc4ad-review.md"}

